\paragraph{Dose-response pattern.}
The relationship between $\beta$ and failure mode is structured across four dose levels: $\beta{=}0.05$ already produces 100\% H7 gaming (single seed), $\beta{=}0.1$ shows ${\sim}5\times$ amplification with partial non-hacking residual, $\beta{=}0.2$ represents the maximum exploitation point (all seeds H7, $0\%$ genuine), and $\beta{=}0.3$ exceeds the training stability boundary with diverse failure modes.
H7 tautological collapse is an absorbing state: once discovered as the global ISR-maximizing exploit, genuine output drops to zero regardless of $\beta$.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Dose-response pattern.}
The relationship between $\beta$ and failure mode is structured across four dose levels: $\beta{=}0.05$ already produces 100\% H7 gaming (single seed), $\beta{=}0.1$ shows ${\sim}5\times$ H7 amplification with partial non-hacking residual, $\beta{=}0.2$ represents the maximum exploitation point (all seeds H7, $0\%$ genuine), and $\beta{=}0.3$ exceeds the training stability boundary with diverse failure modes.
When a seed converges to H7, it is an absorbing state: as the global ISR-maximizing exploit, genuine output drops to zero; however, which seeds converge to H7 vs.\ H1 is stochastic (ISR increases H7 probability but does not determine it).

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Hacking mode switching.}
Under identical reward configurations, different seeds converge to qualitatively different modes (Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}): random $\beta{=}0.1$ ranges from H8-enriched (s44) to H7-dominant (s43) to highest non-hacking (s42); ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ similarly switches between H1-heavy (s42) and near-pure H7 (s43/s44, ${\geq}97\%$).
Single-seed results can therefore misrepresent a reward configuration's hacking profile; multi-seed evaluation with per-mode reporting should be standard.

